WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.950
- Behind me is the
Saint Lawrence Seaway,

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:05.170
one of the largest public works projects
in North America's history.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.780
The seaway was a 1950s expansion
of small canals and waterways

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:13.160
into a huge, navigable path for shipping
between the Atlantic and the Great Lakes,

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.990
along with massive
hydroelectric power plants.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:17.700
That expansion came at a cost, though.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.636
The water is high today, but
when it's low

00:00:19.636 --> 00:00:22.770
you can still see the foundations
of Aultsville, Ontario,

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.460
one of the lost villages that were flooded
to make way for progress.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.130
The residents were told
that they had to move

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.860
and see their old town demolished

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.110
and then drowned beneath
new, higher water.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:36.370
They didn't really have
any choice in the matter.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:39.560
And then some scientists
came along and said:

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.920
if these homes are going to be demolished
and then flooded anyway,

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.460
can we set them on fire first?

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.130
- The St. Lawrence Burns was a project

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:50.130
where they were trying to study
how fires develop in buildings,

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.190
specifically how it affects
the survival of the occupants,

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.480
as well as how fire spreads
from one building to another.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.090
The researchers were scientists
from the Building Research Division

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:02.060
of the National Research
Council of Canada.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.170
They burned a total of eight buildings,

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.410
including six single-family
dwelling homes,

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.380
one two-story school,
and one community hall.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.700
This was a rare opportunity,
because up until this point

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:16.450
most of the research
that was done on fires

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.700
were either from the
aftermaths of real fires

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.560
or they did it on small-scale lab tests.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.550
This, the St. Lawrence Burns,
was really the first time

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:29.260
that scientists were able to conduct in-field, full-scale research

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.540
where they had control over the parameters

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.140
as well as being able to observe the fire
from beginning to end.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.140
- There's a fair argument that, if the buildings
were going to be destroyed anyway,

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.250
why not take the opportunity to get
useful data from them?

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:45.330
Whether it's by fire or by water,
the result was going to be the same.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:48.260
But it's also tough to
ignore the human element.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.870
Homes that had been in
families for generations,

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:53.030
the local school, the community hall,

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:55.830
all given a sort of insult to injury

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.900
by folks coming in from
outside for science.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.210
Many people weren't even
told about the burns.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:02.720
Others chose not to watch.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.100
The volunteer fire chief said
in an interview years later

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.283
that watching the flames was "devastating".

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:10.910
But the results were probably worth it.

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:14.080
- They got a lot of different data,
including things like

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:20.010
carbon monoxide concentration, smoke density,
rise of temperature, oxygen levels.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.610
They mounted a microphone in the bedroom.

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:25.160
They found out that the noise
levels produced by a fire

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:30.100
was not enough to wake a sleeping occupant
when the bedroom door is closed.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.260
The assumption prior to the
studies was that convection

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.410
was the biggest cause of fire spread.

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:37.620
But then it turns out it
was actually radiant heat

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.350
that was more likely the culprit.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:44.650
A combustible wood frame house could lead
to another house 40 feet away to ignite.

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:48.270
And even in a non-combustible
building, it could cause

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.140
another house about
32 feet away to ignite.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:54.600
The St. Lawrence Burns helped change the national building code of Canada,

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.397
and it saved lives.

00:02:56.397 --> 00:02:58.750
- Aultsville has almost passed
out of living memory today.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:01.490
On aerial photos you can
still see ghosts of old roads,

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:04.310
structures that haven't been
worn away by the water yet.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:06.480
But you could see there's
a little bit of Aultsville

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.900
in every modern home, in
everything that's been built

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.930
in the developed world
from the 1960s onwards,

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:16.230
because the results from
the St. Lawrence Burns here

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:19.450
really did change the way
that everything was built.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:21.650
Thank you very much to
Betty Chen from Articulations.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.050
- Where I do videos about art and design.

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:27.650
- You'll want to go and check out her video
about the ISO-standard exit sign.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:29.580
It's not all science and
infrastructure like here,

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.630
but it is a great little channel.

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:32.887
Little?

00:03:32.887 --> 00:03:35.060
- Yeah, that's fine. It's pretty little!

